  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1157 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA quick-moving system will move through the area right at the start \nof the weekend, beginning late Friday Night and lasting into early \nSunday. An upper low will pass just south of us, moving in from the \nwest, bringing precipitation chances to most of our CWA. Snow \nshowers will start over the southwest mountains late Friday Night, \nwith the heaviest accumulations falling into Saturday morning. \nExpected snowfall totals have come down slightly since yesterday, \nwith the system having sped up from previous model runs. The San \nJuans can expected widespread 3-6 inches or so, though a few areas \nat the peaks may see slightly higher amounts. The lower slopes of \nthe southwest mountains, along with the rest of our higher terrain, \nwill generally see 2 inches or less, with some portions of the \nSangres maybe seeing 4-6 at the peaks.\n\nSnow levels look to remain above 8 kft, so the lower elevations will \nbe seeing all rain from this one. Most of the eastern plains should \nsee passing rain showers by the end of the day on Saturday, with the \nheaviest rainfall over our southeast counties thanks to the low \npassing overhead. Meanwhile, the system will also bring in some \ncooler air, with high temperatures falling into the 50s over most of \nthe area, with 40s over some of the valleys and colder temps for the \nmountains.\n\nThe system departs our area on Sunday, leaving drier and cool \nconditions through Monday. \n\nTuesday and onwards, model solutions continue to show a high degree \nof uncertainty. A stronger upper low and associated trough will move \nthrough our area around the middle of next week, bringing more \nwidespread chances for precipitation and cooler temperatures. Snow \nlevels suggest the possibility for snow at lower elevations as well. \nHowever, the path, strength, and speed of the system still varies \nconsiderably between models and individual model runs. Some \nsolutions take the system north or south of us by a considerable \nmargin, while others move the low directly overhead. In general, we \ncan expect the mid-late parts of next week to be colder and more \nactive weather-wise, but beyond that the details are not yet clear \nin the data. Latest trends show the system lasting through at least \nThursday, with another potential system in the works for next \nweekend.",
